---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { ocean } from '../config/images';
import Interlude from '../components/Interlude.astro';

const crumbs = [{ label: 'Start Here', href: '/start-here/' }];

const path = [
  {
    href: '/explore/birth-chart-basics/',
    title: 'What a birth chart actually is',
    body: 'A map of the sky for one moment and place, and the main kinds of information astrologers study inside it.',
    mins: '6 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/explore/the-big-three/',
    title: 'The big three: Sun, Moon, and rising',
    body: 'Three important placements, why they often feel recognizable, and why they are still only part of the chart.',
    mins: '7 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/explore/planets/',
    title: 'The planets: different functions and drives',
    body: 'The planets describe different functions within the chart. Their meaning changes through sign, house, aspects, and context.',
    mins: '10 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/explore/signs/',
    title: 'The signs: styles of expression',
    body: 'Twelve different modes of expression, plus the elements and modalities that reveal relationships among them.',
    mins: '10 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/explore/houses/',
    title: 'The houses: areas of experience',
    body: 'Twelve areas of life that help locate where a planet or pattern may be most visible.',
    mins: '10 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/explore/aspects/',
    title: 'Aspects: how the parts relate',
    body: 'The angular relationships between planets, and one of the clearest places to see why no placement stands alone.',
    mins: '8 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/tools/explore-your-chart/',
    title: 'Put the pieces together',
    body: 'Use the interactive chart explorer to see how planet, sign, house, and aspect add layers to one another. No birth data needed.',
    mins: '5 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/explore/transits/',
    title: 'Understanding transits',
    body: 'How the moving sky forms new relationships with a natal chart, and why timing adds another layer to interpretation.',
    mins: '7 min',
  },
  {
    href: '/explore/misconceptions/',
    title: 'What astrology can and cannot tell you',
    body: 'A closer look at certainty, prediction, sun signs, free will, and some of the assumptions people bring to astrology.',
    mins: '9 min',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Start Here: learn astrology from the beginning"
  description="A guided nine-step path through astrology for beginners: birth charts, Sun, Moon and rising, planets, signs, houses, aspects, transits, and the limits of general interpretation."
  path="/start-here/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">For complete beginners</p>
        <h1>Start here</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Astrology has a large vocabulary, but you do not need to learn it all at once. This is
          the order I would teach it in: first the pieces, then the relationships among them, and
          finally the question that matters most to me—how does all of this work together?
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.dawnWave.src}
        srcset={ocean.dawnWave.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.dawnWave.alt}
        width={ocean.dawnWave.width}
        height={ocean.dawnWave.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Before you begin</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need to arrive already convinced of what astrology can do. You can learn how
        astrologers use the chart, notice what makes sense to you, question what does not, and keep
        your own judgment intact. Curiosity is enough to begin.
      </p>
      <p>
        You also do not need your birth time to start learning, although it becomes important when
        you want to calculate the angles and houses of a particular chart. And the first time
        through, I recommend going in order. Each step gives you something the next one can build on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Throughout these pages I try to distinguish astronomical facts from astrological
        interpretation, historical conventions from modern practice, and general meanings from my
        own synthesis. Those are not all the same kind of claim, and I think readers deserve to
        know the difference.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Interlude
    image={ocean.lightRays}
    eyebrow="No exam at the end"
    heading="Learn the pieces. Then keep asking how they fit."
    body="A definition is useful, but astrology becomes much more interesting when one part of a chart begins modifying, supporting, or complicating another."
  />

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 52rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">The path</p>
      <h2>Nine steps</h2>
      <div style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        {
          path.map((step, i) => (
            <div class="path-step">
              <span class="path-step__num" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</span>
              <div>
                <h3><a href={step.href}>{step.title}</a></h3>
                <p>{step.body}</p>
                <p class="card__meta">{step.mins} read</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top: var(--space-8);">
        <a class="btn btn--accent" href={path[0].href}>Begin with step one</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">When you're ready for more</p>
      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        {
          [
            ['chart', 'Essential Dignities', '/explore/dignities/', 'A traditional framework for considering planetary condition and capability.'],
            ['compass', 'The Four Angles', '/explore/angles/', 'Ascendant, Descendant, Midheaven, IC, and why an accurate birth time matters.'],
            ['book', 'Schools & Traditions', '/explore/schools/', 'A look at several astrological traditions, where they differ, and why many astrologers study broadly.'],
            ['star', 'Chart Patterns', '/explore/chart-patterns/', 'Stelliums, grand trines, T-squares, and the larger configurations that emerge across a chart.'],
            ['moon', 'Current Sky', '/current-sky/', "What's moving overhead right now, with verified dates and Mo's interpretation."],
            ['spark', 'Questions to Bring', '/explore/questions-to-bring/', 'How a good question can make an astrological conversation much more specific.'],
          ].map(([icon, title, href, desc]) => (
            <article class="card" style="gap: var(--space-2);">
              <span style="color: var(--color-teal);">
                <Icon name={icon as any} size={26} />
              </span>
              <h3 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
                <a href={href}>{title}</a>
              </h3>
              <p style="margin: 0;">{desc}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Keep following the pieces."
    body="Explore Astrology goes deeper into the language and techniques. Current Sky shows how I use that language when I look at the cycles overhead now."
    buttonLabel="Explore Astrology"
    buttonHref="/explore/"
    secondaryLabel="See Current Sky"
    secondaryHref="/current-sky/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
